  <Groups>
    <Group elementId="CPLX-8615" textLabel="nucleoside-pyrophosphate kinase 1" type="Complex">
      <Xref identifier="CPLX-8615" dataSource="plantcyc" />
      <Graphics centerX="300" centerY="275" width="140" height="100" textColor="666666" fontName="Arial" fontSize="10" borderColor="9900CC" borderStyle="Dashed" borderWidth="2.0" fillColor="F5E6FF" shapeType="Rectangle" zOrder="-1" />
      <Comment source="SRI, caspi">Nucleoside diphosphate kinase (NDPK) is a ubiquitous housekeeping enzyme that catalyzes the transfer of a &amp;gamma;-phosphoryl group from a nucleoside triphosphate (NTP) to a nucleoside diphosphate (NDP) via a transient phosphorylation of a conserved histidine residue. While all of the eukaryotic NDPKs are homohexamers, some of the prokaryotic enzymes are homotetramers. In plants, NDPKs are involved in responses to heat stress, UV-B light signaling, growth, reactive oxygen species signaling, and phytochrome signaling. |FRAME: TAX-3702| expresses four NDPKs, which have been named NDPK1, NDPK2, NDPK3 and NDPK4. The main difference among the different isoforms involves their regulation. For example. NDPK2 is activated by phytochromes, while NDPK1 is not |CITS: [15465053]|. The NDPK1, NDPK2 and NDPK3 proteins were purified and crystalized, and the crystal structures were reported |CITS: [15465053]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="CPLX-8615" />
      <Property key="ComplexComponents" value="AT4G09320-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="ComponentCount" value="1" />
      <Property key="IsComplex" value="true" />
      <Property key="Catalyzes" value="RXN-33848, RXN-7451, RXN-7450, RXN-7449, RXN-7448, RXN-7447, RXN-7446, RXN-7445" />
      <Property key="CatalyzesCount" value="8" />
      <Property key="^coefficient" value="6" />
      <Property key="InstanceNameTemplate" value="CPLX-*" />
      <Property key="Species" value="ORG-5993" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_15465053" />
